1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,500
They call it the World Ditch.

2
00:00:04,501 --> 00:00:08,000
It's one of the great engineering feats of modern civilization.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
More than 20,000 ships pass through it each year.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Only 100 miles long, it's the most important waterway in the world.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, the Suez Canal on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It's one of the most stunning visuals in the world, a convoy

7
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:49,980
of great ocean-going ships passing through the Sinai

8
00:00:49,981 --> 00:00:52,760
Desert.

9
00:00:52,761 --> 00:00:56,060
For nearly 150 years, the Suez Canal

10
00:00:56,061 --> 00:00:59,020
has connected the Mediterranean with the Red Sea.

11
00:00:59,021 --> 00:01:02,060
It runs for 101 miles through the Isthmus of Suez

12
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:06,160
in northeastern Egypt.

13
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:09,560
It was originally dug out of the desert by hand.

14
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:12,760
Today, the canal is wide enough to accommodate vessels 500

15
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:17,280
meters long, 70 meters wide, and 70 meters deep.

16
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:21,680
There are no locks, because the two seas are the same level.

17
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:23,920
Ships of all shapes, sizes, and flags

18
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:26,280
pass through the canal each year.

19
00:01:26,281 --> 00:01:29,280
Everything from a supertanker or aircraft carrier

20
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:32,880
to the smallest pleasure craft.

21
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:35,880
Since the day it was completed in 1869,

22
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:39,420
the Suez Canal has been a vital link in world trade,

23
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:42,120
providing a shortcut between Europe and Asia,

24
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:46,640
eliminating the long journey around Africa.

25
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:51,140
On the day it opened, the world became smaller.

26
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:54,040
For centuries, Egypt has served as gatekeeper

27
00:01:54,041 --> 00:01:57,380
between two worlds, east and west.

28
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:00,080
Egypt has always been a center for trade

29
00:02:00,180 --> 00:02:03,280
coming from the Far East, which would come to Egypt

30
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:05,980
and then would be divided into the five trade routes going

31
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:11,700
south to the Sudan, west to North Africa, north to Europe,

32
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:13,900
the Ottoman Empire.

33
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,400
And it's a distribution center, if you like.

34
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:21,000
The most direct route from Europe

35
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:23,240
to the Near and Far East was by ship

36
00:02:23,241 --> 00:02:25,780
through the Mediterranean, then overland

37
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:29,740
through the Isthmus of Suez in northeastern Egypt.

38
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:32,840
Passage through the desert was long and difficult.

39
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:35,380
Once a caravan arrived at the Red Sea,

40
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:37,880
goods and passengers were loaded back onto ships

41
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:38,980
for the rest of the journey.

42
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:45,700
Since ancient times, mankind sought

43
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,200
to construct a water passage through Egypt.

44
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:53,240
We know that some kind of a canal did exist

45
00:02:53,241 --> 00:02:58,240
in what is today the Suez Canal region as far back as the 19th

46
00:02:58,241 --> 00:03:03,240
century BC during the time of the ancient Egyptians.

47
00:03:03,241 --> 00:03:07,240
In these ancient times, the Red Sea extended north to what

48
00:03:07,241 --> 00:03:11,240
today are known as the Bitter Lakes and Lake Timsah.

49
00:03:11,241 --> 00:03:16,240
The pharaoh Sesostris I ordered the construction of a canal to

50
00:03:16,241 --> 00:03:19,240
connect these Suez lakes with the Nile River.

51
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:23,240
Cesar Stris and the pharaohs after him managed to create a

52
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:27,240
waterway between the Red Sea and the Mediterranean, just wide

53
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:29,920
enough to accommodate the boats of that era.

54
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:33,260
Egypt had possessions all the way down south in Africa,

55
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:36,760
Egypt, the Egyptian kingdom, the ancient Egyptian kingdom.

56
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:40,800
And in a sense, if that canal existed,

57
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:45,200
it existed mainly for the purposes of Egyptian trade

58
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,840
as opposed to international trade.

59
00:03:47,841 --> 00:03:51,340
The canal of the pharaohs became a well-traveled route

60
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:56,640
and lasted 1,200 years until 664 BC.

61
00:03:56,740 --> 00:04:00,580
By then, desert winds had piled sand between the bitter lakes

62
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:04,400
and the Red Sea, cutting off the route.

63
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:09,400
In 609 BC, the pharaoh Necco tried to excavate a new channel,

64
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:13,200
but more than 100,000 workers were said to have died in the

65
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:17,840
attempt, most likely from starvation and exposed

66
00:04:17,841 --> 00:04:21,380
to the blistering desert heat.

67
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:24,240
A century later, Darius, king of Persia,

68
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:28,380
began working to uncover the lower portion of the canal.

69
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:31,480
The job was completed two centuries later,

70
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:34,760
when Ptolemy II completed a canal that ran all the way

71
00:04:34,860 --> 00:04:37,400
from the Mediterranean to the Red Sea.

72
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:41,460
In a sense, the extension from the Red Sea

73
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:43,840
to the site of what is today Port Said

74
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:47,040
is the Ptolemaic Canal.

75
00:04:47,041 --> 00:04:52,040
itself. It corresponds, if you will, to the Consoros Canal.

76
00:04:55,040 --> 00:05:00,040
During the Roman occupation of Egypt from 30 BC to 14 AD, the

77
00:05:00,041 --> 00:05:05,040
canal of the pharaohs was maintained and improved. During

78
00:05:05,041 --> 00:05:09,040
this period, trade flourished in the region. Greek, Egyptian, and

79
00:05:09,041 --> 00:05:13,040
other merchants passed daily over the desert, both by ship on

80
00:05:13,041 --> 00:05:16,080
on the canal and overland in great caravans.

81
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:20,980
They carried the wealth of the east to trade for Roman gold.

82
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:24,160
In the seventh century, at the time of the Muslim domination

83
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:29,060
of Egypt, it was rebuilt by order of the caliph Omar.

84
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:31,800
The canal remained in use for another century,

85
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:34,160
but then the desert reclaimed it.

86
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:39,000
This time, it stayed buried for 1,000 years.

87
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:41,740
Trade now moved exclusively overland,

88
00:05:41,741 --> 00:05:45,580
via caravan, but there were obstacles.

89
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:48,280
Arab desert raiders were taking a toll,

90
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:50,140
and even if these could be avoided,

91
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:54,480
the pirates of the Red Sea were a constant threat.

92
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:59,220
Then in 1498, the Portuguese explorer Vasco da Gama

93
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:01,700
reached India by sailing for the first time

94
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:04,500
around the Cape of Good Hope.

95
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,540
The route took longer than going overland,

96
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:09,400
but it was considered a great deal safer.

97
00:06:09,401 --> 00:06:11,600
Even with that particular change,

98
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:14,800
a lot of trade continues to go through land.

99
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:18,600
And in a sense, there was always this dream

100
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:22,440
of being able to finally connect the Red Sea

101
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:24,920
to the Mediterranean, and thus in the process

102
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:26,620
reduce the length of the trip.

103
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:32,260
By the end of the 15th century,

104
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:36,160
the Egyptian link in the chain of trade was broken.

105
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:37,800
Trade with the East was dominated

106
00:06:37,801 --> 00:06:41,000
for the next four centuries by the British, Dutch,

107
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:44,840
and Portuguese merchant ships who sailed around the southern tip

108
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:49,740
of Africa, a journey that took four months.

109
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:53,040
In 1850, Europe was in the middle of the Industrial

110
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:57,460
Revolution, undergoing enormous social and economic change.

111
00:06:57,560 --> 00:07:00,360
The old aristocracy was losing its hold,

112
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:05,460
and wealth was pouring into the hands of the new middle class.

113
00:07:05,461 --> 00:07:08,460
Trade with the Far East was no longer a luxury.

114
00:07:08,461 --> 00:07:11,960
Now it was essential for Europe's economic growth.

115
00:07:11,961 --> 00:07:15,960
The factories of England and the continent needed copra, jute,

116
00:07:15,961 --> 00:07:22,460
ore, and cotton. In 1845, the first railway line

117
00:07:22,461 --> 00:07:25,460
in Egypt was completed. It connected the Mediterranean

118
00:07:25,461 --> 00:07:29,460
port of Alexandria with the Red Sea port of Suez.

119
00:07:29,461 --> 00:07:33,460
This service was a boon to passengers, but it did little to

120
00:07:33,461 --> 00:07:36,260
to silence the demand for cargo space.

121
00:07:36,261 --> 00:07:38,460
It's a limit how much a railway line can carry

122
00:07:38,461 --> 00:07:40,460
in terms of passengers and shipping,

123
00:07:40,461 --> 00:07:43,460
whereas through a canal, you can have any number of ships

124
00:07:43,461 --> 00:07:46,460
going through.

125
00:07:46,461 --> 00:07:48,960
At this same time, a maritime revolution

126
00:07:48,961 --> 00:07:50,460
was taking place.

127
00:07:50,461 --> 00:07:53,460
Steamships with screw propellers and iron hulls

128
00:07:53,461 --> 00:07:56,460
were replacing the old wooden sailing ships.

129
00:07:56,461 --> 00:07:59,460
Now there were ships capable of carrying enormous loads

130
00:07:59,461 --> 00:08:01,460
of goods from the east.

131
00:08:01,461 --> 00:08:05,160
But the trip around the Cape still took far too long.

132
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:08,860
The eyes of the world once again fell on the Isthmus of Suez.

133
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:17,200
In 1798, Napoleon and his troops had invaded and occupied Egypt.

134
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:20,440
At that time, the chief French engineer of the expedition,

135
00:08:20,540 --> 00:08:23,980
a man named La Per, surveyed the Isthmus of Suez

136
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:27,460
to determine the feasibility of a canal.

137
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:29,220
The engineer concluded that there

138
00:08:29,221 --> 00:08:31,860
was a 30-foot difference in water level between the

139
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:34,520
Mediterranean and the Red Sea.

140
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,160
Building a canal between them, he reported,

141
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:38,660
would be impossible.

142
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:41,000
That particular fear was completely

143
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:45,500
alleviated by additional surveys that were made in the late

144
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:47,800
1840s.

145
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:51,880
And in a sense, even though the surveys proved that what you're

146
00:08:51,980 --> 00:08:54,320
dealing with is the same water level, it was not until the

147
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:57,680
actual opening of the canal in 1869, almost 20 years later,

148
00:08:57,681 --> 00:09:02,680
that everybody's fears were now proven to be wrong.

149
00:09:02,780 --> 00:09:05,680
A thousand years had passed since the desert sands

150
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:08,220
had covered over the canal of the pharaohs.

151
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:12,120
In that time, Egypt had come to be ruled by many conquerors.

152
00:09:12,220 --> 00:09:15,540
Now, in the mid-19th century, the world was ready and eager

153
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:18,040
for a canal to revive the ancient trade routes

154
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:19,540
for the modern era.

155
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:23,440
But the Ottoman Turks who ruled Egypt were not interested.

156
00:09:23,540 --> 00:09:26,580
Neither was Muhammad Ali, the former Turkish general,

157
00:09:26,581 --> 00:09:28,880
now Viceroy of Egypt.

158
00:09:28,980 --> 00:09:31,780
Muhammad Ali needed to be convinced a canal through the

159
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:34,680
Suez would be in her best interest.

160
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:39,120
In 1854, a well-born young Frenchman named Ferdinand de

161
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:44,540
Lesseps set out to do just that.

162
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:48,940
The present maximum capacity of the Suez Canal is 25,000

163
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:51,240
ships annually.

164
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:55,780
The Suez Canal will return on Modern Marvels.

165
00:09:56,580 --> 00:10:02,580
We now return to the Suez Canal on Modern Marvels.

166
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:13,180
Throughout his rule in the early part of the 19th century,

167
00:10:13,181 --> 00:10:16,340
Muhammad Ali had been urged by his European advisors

168
00:10:16,341 --> 00:10:18,820
to authorize a canal through the Isthmus of Suez.

169
00:10:21,660 --> 00:10:25,780
But the Egyptian leader offered a deaf ear.

170
00:10:25,781 --> 00:10:28,880
Ferdinand de Lesseps, a young French diplomat and engineer,

171
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:31,180
was acquainted with Muhammad Ali and his family.

172
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:35,240
De Lesseps had read the account of Napoleon's expeditions

173
00:10:35,241 --> 00:10:39,180
in Egypt in 1798 and believed strongly

174
00:10:39,181 --> 00:10:41,940
that a canal could and should be built.

175
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:45,360
He also knew that if he wished to remain

176
00:10:45,361 --> 00:10:46,840
in the Viceroy's good graces,

177
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:50,040
he would do well not to mention the subject.

178
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:54,720
Instead, the young consul developed a relationship

179
00:10:54,721 --> 00:10:58,560
with Prince Mohammed Said, the viceroy's son.

180
00:10:58,660 --> 00:11:01,660
The prince had a weight problem, which displeased his father.

181
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:07,160
The son tended to be obese.

182
00:11:09,260 --> 00:11:13,100
So his father, Mohammed Ali, who was very strict,

183
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:15,280
had to put him on a diet.

184
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:17,480
So he would come over to my grandfather's house

185
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:20,320
in order to have decent meals.

186
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:24,080
Moreover, the viceroy wanted him to learn fencing,

187
00:11:24,081 --> 00:11:29,680
horse riding, swimming, all the sports that my grandfather was so good at.

188
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:36,000
And the rumor is that de Lesseps was the only place where the poor corporal and Saïd could get a plate of spaghetti.

189
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:38,860
True or not, it makes for a good story.

190
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:42,260
And so a friendship broke out between the two.

191
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:45,900
And when Saïd became ruler, de Lesseps hurried from France and said,

192
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:48,120
I will make your name famous in the world.

193
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:49,380
He did.

194
00:11:49,381 --> 00:11:55,800
It was during this reunion with his old friend Said in 1854

195
00:11:55,801 --> 00:12:00,160
that de Lesseps began to make his case for building a canal in the Suez.

196
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:04,400
Mohammed Said needed little persuasion.

197
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:10,580
On November 30, 1854, he drafted what came to be known as the first concession,

198
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,080
giving his permission for the creation of a Suez Canal Company.

199
00:12:14,081 --> 00:12:19,080
The first director was to be Ferdinand de Lesseps, whom

200
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:23,260
Said referred to as my devoted friend of high rank and exalted

201
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:24,260
birth.

202
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:25,860
SAID SABRIELA, It is important to remember

203
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:28,800
that de Lesseps was also an entrepreneur, not just simply

204
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:30,000
an engineer.

205
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:32,800
He was somebody who envisioned projects and went out and

206
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:35,260
achieved them.

207
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:38,300
The concession was to be in effect for 99 years

208
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:41,700
from the date of opening, and all the necessary land on the

209
00:12:41,701 --> 00:12:46,440
was to be given to the company by the viceroy himself.

210
00:12:46,540 --> 00:12:49,840
The canal was to be opened to ships of all nations.

211
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:54,740
15% of the profits were to go to the Egyptian government, 75%

212
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:57,940
to the canal company, which would be made up of private

213
00:12:58,040 --> 00:13:01,360
investors, and 10% to the original investors who gave

214
00:13:01,460 --> 00:13:03,360
de Lesseps his working capital.

215
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:07,260
My great grandfather was ambitious,

216
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:10,100
but he was ambitious in the right way.

217
00:13:10,101 --> 00:13:16,100
He was ready to dedicate also energy to all projects

218
00:13:16,200 --> 00:13:20,100
that can bring the idea of progress.

219
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:23,100
He was very close to Egypt.

220
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:28,100
When he saw that finally the moment was ready to do it,

221
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:34,100
he jumped on it, and he made it in such a way that he could

222
00:13:34,200 --> 00:13:38,100
not make any personal fortune on it.

223
00:13:38,101 --> 00:13:51,840
De Lesseps really wanted the canal to be open to all nations without discrimination.

224
00:13:51,841 --> 00:13:57,060
There is an article of concession that says just that.

225
00:13:57,061 --> 00:14:01,980
De Lesseps took pains to make sure the Suez Canal Company would be a truly international

226
00:14:01,981 --> 00:14:03,140
organization.

227
00:14:03,141 --> 00:14:06,100
The first board of directors included representatives of 14

228
00:14:06,101 --> 00:14:10,840
countries. Everything appeared in place to begin construction,

229
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:14,260
but then the British government rose in opposition to the

230
00:14:14,261 --> 00:14:16,560
project. When the British learned that the

231
00:14:16,561 --> 00:14:21,480
canal was going to be dug, their major concern was that it was the

232
00:14:21,481 --> 00:14:25,180
French who were going to control the operation and technically be

233
00:14:25,181 --> 00:14:30,340
in charge. And initially, there was fear that reflecting on their

234
00:14:30,341 --> 00:14:33,020
rivalries with the French of the past, that the French might use

235
00:14:33,021 --> 00:14:35,320
against the British, particularly with respect

236
00:14:35,420 --> 00:14:38,620
to that route to India.

237
00:14:38,720 --> 00:14:40,920
Great Britain's position seemed short-sighted,

238
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:43,260
since they had the largest and most powerful fleet

239
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:46,660
in the world and had the most to gain from an operational

240
00:14:46,760 --> 00:14:49,060
Suez Canal.

241
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:55,480
The canal had one fault.

242
00:14:55,580 --> 00:14:58,580
It was perfect.

243
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:01,780
Perfect for England, but it was not English.

244
00:15:01,781 --> 00:15:05,280
And that's the only reason she was against it.

245
00:15:05,380 --> 00:15:07,980
England could not conceive to allow another country

246
00:15:08,080 --> 00:15:12,620
to have the key to the route to India.

247
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:14,420
Great Britain used her influence

248
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:17,320
in European banking circles to prevent de Lesseps

249
00:15:17,420 --> 00:15:20,340
from securing a loan for the $40 million the canal

250
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:23,840
was estimated to cost.

251
00:15:23,940 --> 00:15:27,240
In desperation, he set up an office in Paris

252
00:15:27,340 --> 00:15:30,380
and set about selling shares in the Suez Canal Company

253
00:15:30,381 --> 00:15:33,280
to anyone willing to buy them.

254
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:36,820
His plan was to sell 400,000 shares of stock

255
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:40,820
at 500 francs each, about $500.

256
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:43,320
To keep the company as international as possible,

257
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:46,360
he limited the number of shares that could be sold in France

258
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:49,640
to 220,000.

259
00:15:49,740 --> 00:15:51,840
The stock sold well.

260
00:15:51,940 --> 00:15:54,940
The purchases were mainly from the middle class, thousands

261
00:15:55,040 --> 00:16:00,380
of them, who bought on average about nine shares each.

262
00:16:00,381 --> 00:16:08,360
Some financiers would say in a mocking way, it's a company of waiters.

263
00:16:10,180 --> 00:16:13,840
And it is true that originally the capital came from modest people.

264
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:20,600
Eventually, very slowly, the Suez shares became valued by important people, and it was good

265
00:16:20,601 --> 00:16:24,200
when your daughter married to have some Suez shares in her dowry.

266
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:30,320
Within a few weeks, de la Sep sold 207,000 shares of the stock.

267
00:16:30,380 --> 00:16:33,720
But he still had nearly 200,000 left unsold.

268
00:16:33,820 --> 00:16:38,020
Now he turned to his old friend, Mohammed Said.

269
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:41,020
The Egyptian ruler agreed to buy 92,000

270
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:42,520
of the remaining shares.

271
00:16:42,620 --> 00:16:44,620
But when the rest of the shares couldn't be sold,

272
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:48,240
de la Sepse took it on his own to announce that Said had

273
00:16:48,340 --> 00:16:50,640
agreed to purchase those too.

274
00:16:50,740 --> 00:16:54,140
DE LA SEPSE WAS A VERY, VERY CLEVER ENTREPRENEUR.

275
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:57,880
He was also a bit of a crook, which comes through very clearly

276
00:16:57,881 --> 00:17:00,420
Clearly, when he declares that the ruler of Egypt

277
00:17:00,421 --> 00:17:02,720
had bought all the unsold shares,

278
00:17:02,721 --> 00:17:04,520
which the ruler of Egypt hadn't bought

279
00:17:04,521 --> 00:17:07,280
because he didn't have the money to buy them,

280
00:17:07,281 --> 00:17:11,700
he simply declared that Said would buy all the unsold shares.

281
00:17:11,701 --> 00:17:15,160
And when Said said, hold it, I never said I would buy them.

282
00:17:15,161 --> 00:17:16,740
He said, would you have me appear a liar

283
00:17:16,741 --> 00:17:18,900
in the eyes of the world?

284
00:17:18,901 --> 00:17:22,580
And Said, being gullible and credulous,

285
00:17:22,581 --> 00:17:27,640
went along with it and had to borrow 10 million pounds

286
00:17:27,641 --> 00:17:30,600
to pay for these shares.

287
00:17:30,601 --> 00:17:34,140
Saeed now owned nearly half interest in the canal,

288
00:17:34,141 --> 00:17:36,680
but to pay for his shares, he had to borrow money

289
00:17:36,681 --> 00:17:39,320
from the same European bankers who had refused

290
00:17:39,321 --> 00:17:42,820
to finance the canal. Only this time,

291
00:17:42,821 --> 00:17:47,460
they were happy to oblige him at usurious interest rates.

292
00:17:47,461 --> 00:17:51,140
Saeed Saeed, Everybody wanted to buy into the loan

293
00:17:51,141 --> 00:17:54,340
because you could borrow money in Europe at 3% and 4%

294
00:17:54,341 --> 00:17:57,700
and then invest it in Egypt and get 12% to 30% return

295
00:17:57,701 --> 00:18:00,880
on your money.

296
00:18:00,881 --> 00:18:02,960
When the money was finally in hand,

297
00:18:02,961 --> 00:18:04,900
the project could begin.

298
00:18:04,901 --> 00:18:08,500
But the financial outlook appeared bleak for Egypt.

299
00:18:08,501 --> 00:18:12,460
Said had recklessly committed, or been pressured into committing,

300
00:18:12,461 --> 00:18:14,580
funds he didn't have.

301
00:18:14,581 --> 00:18:17,900
Construction of the Suez Canal and the financial disintegration

302
00:18:17,901 --> 00:18:22,300
of Egypt began at almost the same moment.

303
00:18:22,301 --> 00:18:27,300
was founded in 1859 as a camp for workers on the Suez Canal.

304
00:18:27,301 --> 00:18:32,300
The Suez Canal will return on Modern Marvels.

305
00:18:32,301 --> 00:18:39,300
We now return to the Suez Canal on Modern Marvels.

306
00:18:39,301 --> 00:18:52,280
Construction of the Suez Canal is a

307
00:18:52,281 --> 00:18:57,280
began on the morning of April 25, 1859.

308
00:18:57,380 --> 00:19:00,180
The engineers had plotted a route from the Mediterranean

309
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:03,400
to the Red Sea, which encompassed Lake Timsa

310
00:19:03,500 --> 00:19:06,060
and the Bitter Lakes.

311
00:19:06,160 --> 00:19:09,360
This limited almost by half the distance that would actually

312
00:19:09,460 --> 00:19:12,460
have to be dug out, just less than 40 miles.

313
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:17,800
The area was a complete desert.

314
00:19:17,801 --> 00:19:21,600
desert.

315
00:19:21,700 --> 00:19:25,100
There was nothing besides a small port in Suez on the Red

316
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:26,400
Sea.

317
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:28,300
Challenges were huge.

318
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:32,040
First of all, to bring manpower to work, then to feed them and

319
00:19:32,140 --> 00:19:35,260
shelter them.

320
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:38,860
90% of the work was just digging in the sand.

321
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:41,360
There were very few rocky zones.

322
00:19:41,460 --> 00:19:45,400
They had to build the Said port, dig the canal, and also

323
00:19:45,401 --> 00:19:50,700
established the cities of Ismailia and Suez.

324
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:53,900
There were no materials to move huge quantities of sand as we

325
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:58,140
could imagine today.

326
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:01,640
Mohamed Said provided the workforce of 60,000

327
00:20:01,740 --> 00:20:05,360
Egyptian peasants, as per the terms of his second concession

328
00:20:05,460 --> 00:20:07,460
to de Lesseps.

329
00:20:07,560 --> 00:20:12,060
20,000 men at a time worked in six-month shifts.

330
00:20:12,061 --> 00:20:19,400
This unwilling workforce was called corvée labor.

331
00:20:19,401 --> 00:20:23,900
Usually, corvée is done on your own region.

332
00:20:23,901 --> 00:20:26,000
But they were now removed from the region,

333
00:20:26,001 --> 00:20:29,720
taken to the Suez Canal, and forced to fend for themselves.

334
00:20:29,721 --> 00:20:32,980
And since their families couldn't be supported,

335
00:20:32,981 --> 00:20:34,220
they followed as well.

336
00:20:34,221 --> 00:20:39,320
So it uprooted the whole population.

337
00:20:39,321 --> 00:20:41,560
Whether the Egyptians were paid for their labor

338
00:20:41,561 --> 00:20:45,160
or even given tools to dig with remains a topic of debate

339
00:20:45,260 --> 00:20:46,800
to this day.

340
00:20:46,900 --> 00:20:50,560
At least 25,000 Egyptians died digging the canal

341
00:20:50,660 --> 00:20:53,500
because the Lesseps neither supplied them with housing,

342
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:56,700
with food, nor even with tools.

343
00:20:56,800 --> 00:20:59,320
They had to dig with whatever tools were available.

344
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:01,520
And you have pictures of them digging, some of them

345
00:21:01,620 --> 00:21:02,820
with their bare hands.

346
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:04,520
It was quite horrendous.

347
00:21:04,620 --> 00:21:06,120
They were paid.

348
00:21:06,220 --> 00:21:07,960
They were paid correctly.

349
00:21:08,060 --> 00:21:10,860
But they didn't have the chance to say that they have another job

350
00:21:10,861 --> 00:21:13,360
They had to do it like a military service.

351
00:21:13,460 --> 00:21:15,100
You know, it was an obligation.

352
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:16,360
Digging it with their bare hands

353
00:21:16,460 --> 00:21:18,500
relates to the unbelievable oppressive conditions

354
00:21:18,600 --> 00:21:23,000
under which the laborers, mostly Egyptians,

355
00:21:23,100 --> 00:21:28,320
they were expected to work about 18 hours a day.

356
00:21:28,420 --> 00:21:32,380
And many of them worked for four, five, and six years

357
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:36,520
before they were finally returned back to their village.

358
00:21:36,620 --> 00:21:39,560
Mohamed Saeed, in addition to donating the labor

359
00:21:39,561 --> 00:21:45,380
the land through which the canal would run, had promised to supply fresh water to the workers.

360
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:51,720
For this, he ordered a separate workforce to dig a fresh water canal from the Nile,

361
00:21:52,100 --> 00:21:56,980
50 miles away. During the five years it took to build the freshwater canal,

362
00:21:57,500 --> 00:22:01,079
water was carried over land to the workers by camels.

363
00:22:01,080 --> 00:22:03,780
The construction plan for the main canal

364
00:22:03,781 --> 00:22:09,200
was to dig out a narrow service ditch all the way from the Mediterranean to the Gulf of Suez.

365
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:13,400
This service ditch, never more than 20 feet wide and 6 feet

366
00:22:13,500 --> 00:22:18,200
deep, would then be widened and dredged out to form the Suez

367
00:22:18,300 --> 00:22:20,800
Canal.

368
00:22:20,900 --> 00:22:24,300
The workforce was split in two, with one team pushing south

369
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:29,220
from Port Said and the other pushing north from Suez.

370
00:22:29,320 --> 00:22:32,420
The Egyptians dug through the desert, following the route of

371
00:22:32,520 --> 00:22:36,360
the pharaohs, the same path dug out by their ancestors, with

372
00:22:36,361 --> 00:22:42,600
ancestors, with the same tools, shovels, baskets, and bare hands.

373
00:22:42,601 --> 00:22:44,760
De Lesseps knew that sooner or later,

374
00:22:44,761 --> 00:22:47,820
he would need heavy machinery to widen out the service ditch

375
00:22:47,821 --> 00:22:50,260
to form the finished canal.

376
00:22:50,261 --> 00:22:52,260
But after three years of digging,

377
00:22:52,261 --> 00:22:55,360
he still had no idea where this equipment would come from

378
00:22:55,361 --> 00:22:57,160
or who would pay for it.

379
00:22:57,161 --> 00:23:00,300
Still, he pressed on, believing that the farther the project

380
00:23:00,301 --> 00:23:04,380
progressed, the more likely it was to attract new financing.

381
00:23:04,381 --> 00:23:09,120
the future would have to take care of itself.

382
00:23:09,121 --> 00:23:11,840
While the digging continued, a harbor was being built

383
00:23:11,841 --> 00:23:13,580
at Port Said.

384
00:23:13,581 --> 00:23:16,060
Two stone breakwaters were formed reaching out

385
00:23:16,061 --> 00:23:18,060
into the Mediterranean.

386
00:23:18,061 --> 00:23:22,140
30,000 huge stones, each weighing more than 20 tons,

387
00:23:22,141 --> 00:23:24,880
were lowered into the sea.

388
00:23:24,881 --> 00:23:29,040
In 1863, nine years after he granted the Suez Canal

389
00:23:29,041 --> 00:23:32,140
concession to his friend Ferdinand de Lesseps,

390
00:23:32,141 --> 00:23:37,140
died. He was succeeded as viceroy by his nephew, Ismail, who was

391
00:23:37,141 --> 00:23:42,140
soon faced with a new crisis. Slavery was a topic very much in

392
00:23:42,141 --> 00:23:45,140
the news. The United States was at that moment

393
00:23:45,141 --> 00:23:48,140
fighting a civil war over the issue, and world opinion was

394
00:23:48,141 --> 00:23:53,140
very much with the abolitionist North. Great Britain seized the

395
00:23:53,141 --> 00:23:57,140
opportunity and accused Egypt of using slave labor to build the

396
00:23:57,141 --> 00:24:01,540
to build the canal. The British, to be sure,

397
00:24:01,640 --> 00:24:06,480
to unbalance the company, made an international campaign

398
00:24:06,580 --> 00:24:10,760
against the corvée. So finally, the corvée has been

399
00:24:10,860 --> 00:24:16,400
abolished everywhere in Egypt, including the Suez Canal

400
00:24:16,500 --> 00:24:21,700
work site. In the spring of 1864,

401
00:24:21,800 --> 00:24:25,300
Ismail reluctantly sent his workforce home.

402
00:24:25,301 --> 00:24:28,100
But this move only compounded his troubles.

403
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:30,140
Egypt's contract with de la Sepz

404
00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:34,540
and the Canal Company required Ismail to supply the labor.

405
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:36,840
If he couldn't supply Corvée labor,

406
00:24:36,940 --> 00:24:40,440
he would have to provide the cash to hire paid workers

407
00:24:40,540 --> 00:24:42,760
or buy dredging machines.

408
00:24:42,860 --> 00:24:45,660
But he had no cash.

409
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:48,060
Now, like his uncle before him, he

410
00:24:48,160 --> 00:24:51,300
was forced to borrow more money from European bankers

411
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:54,560
at high interest rates.

412
00:24:54,561 --> 00:24:57,760
De Lesseps used the money to buy new dredging equipment

413
00:24:57,860 --> 00:24:58,560
from France.

414
00:25:02,260 --> 00:25:05,060
No precedent for the dredges existed,

415
00:25:05,160 --> 00:25:08,000
since the world had never seen an engineering problem like this

416
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:09,400
before.

417
00:25:09,500 --> 00:25:12,820
The machines were steam powered and set up on barges

418
00:25:12,920 --> 00:25:14,420
in the canal.

419
00:25:14,520 --> 00:25:18,220
From wooden towers, an endless chain of buckets scooped mud

420
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:20,360
from the canal bottom.

421
00:25:20,460 --> 00:25:23,760
Long shoots deposited the mud on the banks of the canal

422
00:25:23,761 --> 00:25:27,360
or onto other barges that carried it out to sea.

423
00:25:27,460 --> 00:25:29,760
The dredges followed the path of the service ditch,

424
00:25:29,860 --> 00:25:31,260
widening it out.

425
00:25:31,360 --> 00:25:34,900
Dredging was so much faster and cheaper than dry excavation

426
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,200
that the terrain was artificially flooded

427
00:25:37,300 --> 00:25:41,120
and dredged whenever possible.

428
00:25:41,220 --> 00:25:43,820
These dredges were capable of digging out the canal

429
00:25:43,920 --> 00:25:48,220
at an astounding rate of 6 million cubic feet a month.

430
00:25:48,320 --> 00:25:52,220
Progress on the canal quickened dramatically.

431
00:25:52,221 --> 00:25:58,640
Early fears that the canal once dug would immediately fill back up with drifting sand proved unfounded.

432
00:25:59,720 --> 00:26:05,760
The mud from the bottom of the canal made good cement for the banks, baked rock hard by the sun.

433
00:26:08,820 --> 00:26:11,720
The shape of the canal is a trapezoid.

434
00:26:13,380 --> 00:26:20,920
You have the banks, then flat places, then it angles to the bottom, and the bottom is flat.

435
00:26:22,220 --> 00:26:25,720
By 1869, after 10 years of digging,

436
00:26:25,820 --> 00:26:28,160
the canal was large enough to accommodate

437
00:26:28,260 --> 00:26:30,220
ocean-going vessels.

438
00:26:30,320 --> 00:26:34,060
On the day it opened, the Suez Canal was 26 feet deep,

439
00:26:34,160 --> 00:26:37,560
with a minimum bottom width of 72 feet and a surface width

440
00:26:37,660 --> 00:26:40,980
of some 175 feet.

441
00:26:41,080 --> 00:26:43,180
During the last year of construction,

442
00:26:43,280 --> 00:26:46,380
new harbors and towns were completed at Port Said and

443
00:26:46,480 --> 00:26:47,680
Ismailia.

444
00:26:47,780 --> 00:26:52,220
The freshwater canal extended the length of the waterway.

445
00:26:52,221 --> 00:26:58,620
On August 15, 1869, the Red Sea flowed along the channel from

446
00:26:58,621 --> 00:27:02,620
Suez and poured into the bitter lakes, blending with water from

447
00:27:02,621 --> 00:27:05,660
the Mediterranean. The opening of the Suez Canal

448
00:27:05,661 --> 00:27:13,100
in 1869 was an event of great proportions.

449
00:27:13,101 --> 00:27:15,580
International guests were invited from all over the world to

450
00:27:15,581 --> 00:27:20,140
attend the ceremony itself. The celebration lasted for

451
00:27:20,141 --> 00:27:23,640
for days. Ismail paid for the lavish festivities

452
00:27:23,641 --> 00:27:27,640
with borrowed funds. He spent 15 million pounds,

453
00:27:27,641 --> 00:27:30,380
which at that time was a great deal of money, built the Cairo

454
00:27:30,381 --> 00:27:33,980
Opera House. Verdi wrote Aida, which wasn't ready in time for

455
00:27:33,981 --> 00:27:37,600
the opening of the canal, but was later on.

456
00:27:37,601 --> 00:27:42,800
On November 17, 1869, a royal yacht carrying the Empress

457
00:27:42,801 --> 00:27:46,800
Eugénie of France, Ferdinand de Lesseps, and other dignitaries

458
00:27:46,801 --> 00:27:50,700
steamed into the completed Suez Canal.

459
00:27:50,800 --> 00:27:54,700
The world had its waterway, but for Egypt, the price tag

460
00:27:54,800 --> 00:27:57,740
would be higher than she ever could have imagined.

461
00:27:57,840 --> 00:28:01,740
America's Statue of Liberty was originally intended to stand

462
00:28:01,840 --> 00:28:05,360
in Port Said at the entrance to the Suez Canal,

463
00:28:05,460 --> 00:28:09,460
but Ismail decided the project was too expensive.

464
00:28:09,560 --> 00:28:12,860
The Suez Canal will return on Modern Marvels.

465
00:28:16,800 --> 00:28:20,500
We now return to the Suez Canal on Modern Marvels.

466
00:28:24,500 --> 00:28:28,540
The Suez Canal's opening in 1869 was well-timed,

467
00:28:28,541 --> 00:28:31,400
in that it coincided with the world manufacturing boom

468
00:28:31,401 --> 00:28:35,200
accompanying the Industrial Revolution.

469
00:28:35,201 --> 00:28:37,200
There were suddenly more products for sale

470
00:28:37,201 --> 00:28:40,000
than at any previous time in history.

471
00:28:40,001 --> 00:28:42,500
Shipments of products between East and West

472
00:28:42,501 --> 00:28:45,500
that once took months to go around the Cape of Good Hope

473
00:28:45,501 --> 00:28:49,500
were now reaching their destinations in short weeks.

474
00:28:49,600 --> 00:28:53,240
As transportation costs lessened, prices dropped.

475
00:28:53,340 --> 00:28:57,180
A new emerging middle class, flush with their factory wages,

476
00:28:57,280 --> 00:29:00,840
found they could now afford what once had been out of reach.

477
00:29:00,940 --> 00:29:04,820
It facilitated trade and commerce enormously.

478
00:29:04,920 --> 00:29:09,360
It allowed a number of European investors

479
00:29:09,460 --> 00:29:12,620
to make enormous quantities of money.

480
00:29:12,621 --> 00:29:17,200
It allowed for the smooth sailing to India so that England

481
00:29:17,300 --> 00:29:21,200
could send its armies to India much easier than before,

482
00:29:21,300 --> 00:29:24,700
and the French to Indochina.

483
00:29:24,800 --> 00:29:27,640
The name Ferdinand de Lesseps was being spoken

484
00:29:27,740 --> 00:29:29,600
all over the world.

485
00:29:29,700 --> 00:29:32,740
His canal was hailed as a technological triumph,

486
00:29:32,840 --> 00:29:36,580
and just as he had predicted, it was benefiting all nations,

487
00:29:36,680 --> 00:29:38,820
all except Egypt.

488
00:29:42,620 --> 00:29:49,620
I'm not sure that the Suez Canal brought a lot to Egypt itself.

489
00:29:49,621 --> 00:29:53,620
Ships would pass through, maybe pick up coal at Port Said, but

490
00:29:53,621 --> 00:29:57,620
they would not bring important resources to Egypt.

491
00:29:57,621 --> 00:30:00,620
I do not think very honestly that the canal brought economic

492
00:30:00,621 --> 00:30:03,620
prosperity to Egypt.

493
00:30:03,621 --> 00:30:09,620
By 1875, Egypt was facing bankruptcy, and no financier

494
00:30:09,621 --> 00:30:12,820
No financier in Europe would loan her any more money.

495
00:30:12,821 --> 00:30:15,960
Her last major source of income, her cotton crop,

496
00:30:15,961 --> 00:30:17,820
had been mortgaged a decade earlier

497
00:30:17,821 --> 00:30:19,660
to pay for canal dredges.

498
00:30:19,661 --> 00:30:22,620
Now those payments were due, and her only other source

499
00:30:22,621 --> 00:30:25,880
of income was her half interest in the Suez Canal.

500
00:30:25,881 --> 00:30:28,660
And even though 5,000 ships were passing through the canal

501
00:30:28,661 --> 00:30:31,180
annually, her portion of the collected tolls

502
00:30:31,181 --> 00:30:34,560
was not nearly enough to pay off her creditors.

503
00:30:34,561 --> 00:30:38,620
Out of desperation, Ismail sought a buyer for Egypt shares

504
00:30:38,621 --> 00:30:40,520
in the Suez Canal Company.

505
00:30:40,521 --> 00:30:43,660
One quickly appeared in the form of Egypt's old nemesis,

506
00:30:43,661 --> 00:30:45,660
the British government.

507
00:30:45,661 --> 00:30:48,220
When Benjamin Disraeli, the British prime minister,

508
00:30:48,221 --> 00:30:53,160
heard Ismail's shares were for sale, he acted swiftly.

509
00:30:53,161 --> 00:30:56,780
On a weekend without seeking authorization from parliament,

510
00:30:56,781 --> 00:31:02,620
Disraeli offered Egypt $20 million for the shares.

511
00:31:02,621 --> 00:31:05,120
Ismail accepted.

512
00:31:05,121 --> 00:31:07,680
Disraeli immediately sent a message to the queen,

513
00:31:07,681 --> 00:31:13,020
simply, you have it, madam.

514
00:31:13,120 --> 00:31:16,020
England, the nation who had done everything she could

515
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:18,720
to prevent the Suez Canal from being built,

516
00:31:18,820 --> 00:31:20,920
now owned half of it.

517
00:31:21,020 --> 00:31:23,320
Egypt, whose people had labored for years

518
00:31:23,420 --> 00:31:26,140
to dig out the canal, whose commitment to the project

519
00:31:26,240 --> 00:31:28,640
had plunged her into bankruptcy, no longer

520
00:31:28,740 --> 00:31:31,940
owned any part of the venture.

521
00:31:32,040 --> 00:31:34,480
This transaction also marked the beginning of the end

522
00:31:34,481 --> 00:31:39,480
of the end for Ismail, who was deposed a short time afterward.

523
00:31:39,481 --> 00:31:42,180
Within 15 years, the shares Disraeli bought

524
00:31:42,181 --> 00:31:46,020
would be worth seven times what he paid for them.

525
00:31:46,021 --> 00:31:48,220
Great Britain's purchase of the canal shares

526
00:31:48,221 --> 00:31:52,080
gave her a foot in the door into Egypt's internal affairs.

527
00:31:52,081 --> 00:31:57,020
Only a few years later, she would barge her way through.

528
00:31:57,021 --> 00:31:59,980
In 1882, nationalist Egyptians revolted

529
00:31:59,981 --> 00:32:03,920
against Khediv Tawfig, Ismail's successor.

530
00:32:03,921 --> 00:32:07,120
The rebel leaders wanted freedom from the outside governments

531
00:32:07,220 --> 00:32:10,160
which had controlled Egypt for centuries.

532
00:32:10,260 --> 00:32:14,660
Seeking to protect their investment, the British invaded.

533
00:32:14,760 --> 00:32:19,900
The rebellion was quickly put down, but the British stayed.

534
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:23,140
The British colonized Egypt because of the canal.

535
00:32:23,240 --> 00:32:26,340
They used the excuse that there was a nationalist movement

536
00:32:26,440 --> 00:32:27,540
in Egypt.

537
00:32:27,640 --> 00:32:29,640
The real reason is they were afraid the canal

538
00:32:29,740 --> 00:32:31,980
would be blocked.

539
00:32:31,981 --> 00:32:36,820
The British occupied Egypt for the next 75 years.

540
00:32:36,920 --> 00:32:40,020
The Suez Canal would prove to be a great strategic asset

541
00:32:40,120 --> 00:32:43,120
to Great Britain in both world wars.

542
00:32:43,220 --> 00:32:46,020
The British have a superb navy.

543
00:32:46,120 --> 00:32:47,720
They don't have manpower.

544
00:32:47,820 --> 00:32:51,540
They need manpower from their colonies to fight wars.

545
00:32:51,640 --> 00:32:53,640
And the manpower comes through the Suez Canal

546
00:32:53,740 --> 00:32:56,280
because it comes from Australia, New Zealand.

547
00:32:56,380 --> 00:33:01,980
They also had all the Indians and Burmese in their army.

548
00:33:01,981 --> 00:33:04,320
You couldn't do it without the Suez Canal.

549
00:33:04,321 --> 00:33:08,280
Now, in theory, the canal is an open international waterway.

550
00:33:08,281 --> 00:33:12,020
In both wars, it was closed to the other side.

551
00:33:12,021 --> 00:33:15,400
But without the canal, nothing would have reached England.

552
00:33:15,401 --> 00:33:19,000
It wouldn't have been able to fight a war.

553
00:33:19,001 --> 00:33:20,940
The first half of the 20th century

554
00:33:20,941 --> 00:33:24,400
had seen the Egyptian population double.

555
00:33:24,401 --> 00:33:27,140
With few natural resources to draw from,

556
00:33:27,141 --> 00:33:29,540
the standard of living declined.

557
00:33:29,541 --> 00:33:33,680
But through it all, the canal prospered.

558
00:33:33,681 --> 00:33:37,340
In 1936, Britain ended its official control

559
00:33:37,341 --> 00:33:41,340
of the Egyptian government, but remained a solid presence there,

560
00:33:41,341 --> 00:33:44,600
backing their puppet, King Farouk.

561
00:33:44,601 --> 00:33:47,780
Then in 1952, King Farouk was deposed

562
00:33:47,781 --> 00:33:51,340
in a bloodless coup carried out by a group of army officers.

563
00:33:51,341 --> 00:33:53,600
Their leader, Gamal Abdul Nasser,

564
00:33:53,601 --> 00:33:55,780
took over as head of the new government,

565
00:33:55,781 --> 00:33:58,380
becoming the first true Egyptian to rule Egypt

566
00:33:58,381 --> 00:34:02,380
of the 6th century BC. Within a few short years,

567
00:34:02,381 --> 00:34:05,380
his destiny and that of the Suez Canal

568
00:34:05,381 --> 00:34:07,380
would become inextricably linked.

569
00:34:07,381 --> 00:34:10,380
Nasser was now the head of an impoverished nation,

570
00:34:10,381 --> 00:34:12,380
which lacked enough cultivatable soil

571
00:34:12,381 --> 00:34:15,380
to feed its rapidly increasing population.

572
00:34:15,381 --> 00:34:17,380
The answer, he and others believed,

573
00:34:17,381 --> 00:34:21,380
was to dam the River Nile at Aswan in southern Egypt,

574
00:34:21,381 --> 00:34:23,380
thereby creating a means to increase

575
00:34:23,381 --> 00:34:26,380
the agricultural output of the Nile Valley.

576
00:34:26,381 --> 00:34:29,520
Not only would food production increase by an estimated one

577
00:34:29,620 --> 00:34:33,180
third, but the hydroelectric plant in the proposed dam

578
00:34:33,280 --> 00:34:36,620
would bring electricity to millions of rural Egyptians,

579
00:34:36,720 --> 00:34:41,220
giving them a boost into the 20th century.

580
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:44,840
Without the dam, the outlook for Egypt was bleak.

581
00:34:44,940 --> 00:34:48,040
The prospect of mass starvation within the foreseeable future

582
00:34:48,140 --> 00:34:50,840
was very real.

583
00:34:50,940 --> 00:34:53,640
But the cost of the Aswan Dam was estimated

584
00:34:53,641 --> 00:34:57,240
estimated to be a billion dollars.

585
00:34:57,340 --> 00:34:59,980
Like his predecessors a century before,

586
00:35:00,080 --> 00:35:03,480
Nasser turned to the wealthy nations for a loan.

587
00:35:03,580 --> 00:35:05,980
But the Cold War was in full swing,

588
00:35:06,080 --> 00:35:09,280
and the Western powers, specifically the United States,

589
00:35:09,380 --> 00:35:12,500
believed Nasser had sympathies for the Soviet bloc.

590
00:35:12,600 --> 00:35:15,700
Nasser was not a communist, hated the communists.

591
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:19,140
He was determined not to have communist influence come

592
00:35:19,240 --> 00:35:22,140
into Egypt.

593
00:35:22,141 --> 00:35:27,240
On July 19, 1956, the Egyptian president's request for a loan

594
00:35:27,241 --> 00:35:28,980
was rejected.

595
00:35:28,981 --> 00:35:32,280
Angry and humiliated, Nasser played what he believed

596
00:35:32,281 --> 00:35:33,920
was his final card.

597
00:35:33,921 --> 00:35:36,380
One week after the World Bank's decision,

598
00:35:36,381 --> 00:35:39,720
Egyptian troops seized the only reliable source of revenue

599
00:35:39,721 --> 00:35:41,060
in Egypt.

600
00:35:41,061 --> 00:35:43,900
On the 26th of July, Gamal Abdel Nasser

601
00:35:43,901 --> 00:35:49,260
nationalized the Suez Canal and the argument he put forward,

602
00:35:49,261 --> 00:35:52,360
which was enthusiastically received by the Egyptians,

603
00:35:52,900 --> 00:35:55,920
was that this would provide the cash for the high dam.

604
00:35:57,280 --> 00:36:00,860
The canal's owners, France and Britain, were enraged.

605
00:36:01,440 --> 00:36:05,480
In concert with Israeli forces, the two countries invaded Egypt

606
00:36:05,481 --> 00:36:07,320
and occupied the canal territory.

607
00:36:08,140 --> 00:36:10,780
It appeared that Egypt would once again fall

608
00:36:10,781 --> 00:36:12,540
under the control of foreign nations.

609
00:36:13,400 --> 00:36:17,180
But then, a pair of unlikely champions emerged in her behalf.

610
00:36:17,181 --> 00:36:30,987
The Americans and the Soviets felt that it was inappropriate for the British and the French to occupy the Suez Canal region without complete and full consultation with

611
00:36:30,988 --> 00:36:31,688
them.

612
00:36:31,689 --> 00:36:34,540
Of course, we have to remember that this was the British Empire.

613
00:36:34,541 --> 00:36:36,160
This was still the French Empire.

614
00:36:36,161 --> 00:36:39,300
Both countries were operating from an imperial regime mentality.

615
00:36:39,301 --> 00:36:41,740
Egypt's triumph in nationalizing the Suez Canal

616
00:36:41,840 --> 00:36:47,240
was, in a sense, the moment that ended the reign of Britain

617
00:36:47,340 --> 00:36:51,740
over the world as a British empire.

618
00:36:51,840 --> 00:36:54,640
Pressured by the United States and the Soviet Union,

619
00:36:54,740 --> 00:36:57,060
the United Nations ordered Britain and France

620
00:36:57,160 --> 00:36:59,860
to withdraw from the Canal Zone.

621
00:36:59,960 --> 00:37:03,560
Israel was ordered to leave the Sinai Peninsula.

622
00:37:03,660 --> 00:37:06,360
The withdrawal was humiliating for these countries,

623
00:37:06,361 --> 00:37:09,160
as was the United Nations' subsequent pronouncement

624
00:37:09,161 --> 00:37:13,160
that the canal belonged to Egypt.

625
00:37:13,161 --> 00:37:16,160
To some observers, the conflict surrounding the canal seizure

626
00:37:16,161 --> 00:37:21,160
in 1956 seemed pointless, since the original 99-year lease

627
00:37:21,161 --> 00:37:24,160
granted by Mohammed Saeed to Ferdinand de Lesseps

628
00:37:24,161 --> 00:37:28,160
was set to expire in 1968. At that time,

629
00:37:28,161 --> 00:37:33,160
ownership of the canal would have reverted to Egypt anyway.

630
00:37:33,161 --> 00:37:36,160
More than a century had passed since Ferdinand de Lesseps

631
00:37:36,161 --> 00:37:40,900
Ferdinand de Lesseps began his campaign to build the Suez Canal.

632
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:44,000
The statue erected in his honor at the entrance to the canal

633
00:37:44,100 --> 00:37:47,000
in Port Said had, to many Egyptians,

634
00:37:47,100 --> 00:37:50,600
become a symbol of Egypt's long and bitter history of subjugation

635
00:37:50,700 --> 00:37:53,820
to foreign powers.

636
00:37:53,920 --> 00:37:56,620
A few months after Egypt seized the canal,

637
00:37:56,720 --> 00:38:00,160
nationalist Egyptians used dynamite to knock the statue

638
00:38:00,260 --> 00:38:01,820
off its pedestal.

639
00:38:01,920 --> 00:38:05,520
It had taken a century, but now, for the first time,

640
00:38:05,521 --> 00:38:09,860
Egypt owned it all. Following the Arab-Israeli War in

641
00:38:09,861 --> 00:38:14,520
1948, the Jewish state was the only nation on Earth barred from

642
00:38:14,521 --> 00:38:18,960
using the Suez Canal. The Suez Canal will return on

643
00:38:18,961 --> 00:38:25,780
Modern Marvels. We now return to the Suez Canal

644
00:38:25,781 --> 00:38:28,280
on Modern Marvels.

645
00:38:35,520 --> 00:38:39,360
Gamal Abdel Nasser got his Aswan Dam,

646
00:38:39,460 --> 00:38:41,860
and he didn't need the revenues from the Suez Canal

647
00:38:41,960 --> 00:38:43,060
to pay for it.

648
00:38:43,160 --> 00:38:45,360
Much to the dismay of the Western powers,

649
00:38:45,460 --> 00:38:48,260
the Soviets stepped forward and put up the money

650
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:50,260
for construction.

651
00:38:50,360 --> 00:38:52,280
Egypt was on a roll.

652
00:38:52,380 --> 00:38:55,180
Within a decade, she had overthrown the monarchy,

653
00:38:55,280 --> 00:38:59,120
negotiated her independence, seized control of the Suez

654
00:38:59,220 --> 00:39:02,580
Canal, and begun construction on a dam that would eventually

655
00:39:02,581 --> 00:39:06,520
that would eventually double her agricultural output.

656
00:39:06,620 --> 00:39:10,080
The nation that for centuries had been ruled by foreigners

657
00:39:10,180 --> 00:39:14,020
now stood firmly at the helm of its own destiny.

658
00:39:14,120 --> 00:39:18,820
By 1966, over 20,000 ships were moving through the canal

659
00:39:18,920 --> 00:39:19,840
annually.

660
00:39:19,940 --> 00:39:23,340
They carried 242 million tons of cargo

661
00:39:23,440 --> 00:39:25,840
and 40% of Europe's oil imports.

662
00:39:25,841 --> 00:39:33,680
Then, in 1967, war broke out with Israel.

663
00:39:33,780 --> 00:39:36,480
The conflict lasted only six days,

664
00:39:36,580 --> 00:39:40,180
but it left Israel in control of the Sinai Peninsula just

665
00:39:40,280 --> 00:39:42,080
across the canal.

666
00:39:42,180 --> 00:39:44,400
The Egyptians sunk ships in the canal

667
00:39:44,500 --> 00:39:47,200
to keep out Israeli gunboats, turning it

668
00:39:47,300 --> 00:39:49,100
into a watery no man's land.

669
00:39:52,140 --> 00:39:55,840
The canal remained closed for eight years.

670
00:39:55,841 --> 00:39:59,140
The loss to Egypt was devastating, $2 billion

671
00:39:59,240 --> 00:40:02,580
in uncollected tolls.

672
00:40:02,680 --> 00:40:06,680
When the canal opened in 1869, the cross section of the waterway

673
00:40:06,780 --> 00:40:09,280
measured 300 square meters.

674
00:40:09,380 --> 00:40:13,400
Over the next 80 years, it was enlarged seven times.

675
00:40:13,500 --> 00:40:16,100
When it was nationalized in 1956,

676
00:40:16,200 --> 00:40:19,440
the canal measured 1,200 square meters.

677
00:40:19,540 --> 00:40:22,200
The new Egyptian owners then increased it again

678
00:40:22,300 --> 00:40:24,700
to 1,800 square meters.

679
00:40:24,701 --> 00:40:27,340
When the canal reopened in 1975,

680
00:40:27,440 --> 00:40:30,480
the size of an average ocean-going freighter or tanker

681
00:40:30,580 --> 00:40:33,080
had increased substantially.

682
00:40:33,180 --> 00:40:37,020
Since that time, the canal has undergone constant expansion.

683
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:39,940
Until today, it can accommodate 97%

684
00:40:40,040 --> 00:40:42,960
of the world's commercial fleet.

685
00:40:43,060 --> 00:40:44,980
Even when it's not being expanded,

686
00:40:45,080 --> 00:40:47,120
the canal undergoes daily dredging

687
00:40:47,220 --> 00:40:50,700
to keep up with erosion in the constantly blowing desert sands.

688
00:40:54,700 --> 00:40:58,540
Fewer ships travel through the canal today than did 30 years

689
00:40:58,640 --> 00:41:02,940
ago, because today's ships carry much larger cargoes.

690
00:41:03,040 --> 00:41:04,940
When he was planning the Suez Canal,

691
00:41:05,040 --> 00:41:07,940
Ferdinand de Lesseps envisioned a truly international

692
00:41:08,040 --> 00:41:11,460
enterprise, open to ships of all flags.

693
00:41:11,560 --> 00:41:14,760
Since the canal's reopening in 1975,

694
00:41:14,860 --> 00:41:17,560
it has remained true to that vision.

695
00:41:17,660 --> 00:41:19,760
Egypt owns and operates the canal,

696
00:41:19,860 --> 00:41:24,700
but in a sense, it belongs to the world.

697
00:41:24,701 --> 00:41:30,520
Each morning, well before dawn, ships gather at the northern and southern gates to the Suez Canal.

698
00:41:31,520 --> 00:41:33,645
Here, they are arranged in convoys.

699
00:41:33,646 --> 00:41:36,680
The canal is only wide enough for one-way traffic,

700
00:41:36,940 --> 00:41:40,080
but there are bypasses similar to railroad sightings.

701
00:41:41,680 --> 00:41:46,600
Each ship is assigned English-speaking Egyptian pilots for the 15-hour passage.

702
00:41:47,860 --> 00:41:52,220
Each vessel passing through the canal is monitored from this control station

703
00:41:52,221 --> 00:41:55,520
at the Suez Canal headquarters in Ismailia.

704
00:41:55,620 --> 00:41:58,360
Downstairs from the control room is the canal pilot training

705
00:41:58,460 --> 00:42:00,460
center and wheelhouse simulator.

706
00:42:00,560 --> 00:42:01,960
STEDY COURSES 350, sir.

707
00:42:02,060 --> 00:42:02,860
STEDY.

708
00:42:02,960 --> 00:42:04,660
STEDY 350.

709
00:42:04,760 --> 00:42:07,660
Each canal pilot takes a refresher course in the simulator

710
00:42:07,760 --> 00:42:09,080
three times a year.

711
00:42:09,180 --> 00:42:12,380
This simulator is designed to meet all demands

712
00:42:12,480 --> 00:42:16,420
of the navigator officers and pilots

713
00:42:16,520 --> 00:42:21,820
for training in radar plotting, collision avoidance,

714
00:42:21,821 --> 00:42:27,760
maneuvering, navigation, and updating their skills.

715
00:42:31,360 --> 00:42:35,060
In 1869, the year the Suez Canal opened,

716
00:42:35,160 --> 00:42:37,560
its original architect, Ferdinand de Lesseps,

717
00:42:37,660 --> 00:42:39,580
married for the second time.

718
00:42:39,680 --> 00:42:42,280
He was by then 64 years old.

719
00:42:42,380 --> 00:42:46,620
He and his young wife proceeded to have 11 children.

720
00:42:46,720 --> 00:42:49,320
10 years later, at the age of 74,

721
00:42:49,321 --> 00:42:52,920
He began work on a project to build a canal in Panama,

722
00:42:52,921 --> 00:42:55,920
but found he no longer had the stamina.

723
00:42:55,921 --> 00:42:58,720
Others completed what he began.

724
00:42:58,721 --> 00:43:03,820
He died in 1894 at the age of 89.

725
00:43:03,821 --> 00:43:05,780
The Suez Canal today has turned out

726
00:43:05,781 --> 00:43:08,620
to be just what de Lesseps had hoped for,

727
00:43:08,621 --> 00:43:13,320
a path of trade and communication open to all.

728
00:43:13,321 --> 00:43:16,560
It is unfortunate that, in spite of his vision and efforts,

729
00:43:16,561 --> 00:43:20,960
His name became synonymous in the Egyptian psyche with centuries of

730
00:43:20,961 --> 00:43:25,500
colonial rule and the image of outsiders exploiting a poor country

731
00:43:25,501 --> 00:43:30,940
and her few natural resources. But the fact remains that de

732
00:43:30,941 --> 00:43:35,120
Lesseps, despite his sometimes questionable methods, was the

733
00:43:35,121 --> 00:43:38,400
visionary and prime mover of the Suez Canal excavation.

734
00:43:39,300 --> 00:43:42,320
Without his energy and determination, the project might

735
00:43:42,321 --> 00:43:45,220
not have been attempted for decades or at all.

736
00:43:45,221 --> 00:43:52,220
The Suez Canal remains today an amazing enterprise

737
00:43:52,320 --> 00:43:54,220
because it brings men together.

738
00:43:54,320 --> 00:43:56,960
It was a generous enterprise.

739
00:43:57,060 --> 00:43:59,060
De Lesseps was bankrupt.

740
00:43:59,160 --> 00:44:00,760
He did not keep any money.

741
00:44:00,860 --> 00:44:03,180
He was poor at the end of his life.

742
00:44:03,280 --> 00:44:05,180
He was not materialistic.

743
00:44:05,280 --> 00:44:07,580
I think the great thing of the Suez Canal

744
00:44:07,680 --> 00:44:11,120
is precisely that it is a tie between men.

745
00:44:11,121 --> 00:44:16,660
between men. The bronze statue of Ferdinand

746
00:44:16,661 --> 00:44:20,120
de Lesseps no longer stands at the Port Said entrance to the

747
00:44:20,121 --> 00:44:23,160
canal, but it was never destroyed.

748
00:44:23,161 --> 00:44:26,900
It's tucked away on a service inlet just across the harbor,

749
00:44:26,901 --> 00:44:30,800
as if waiting for the tide of opinion to turn so it might one

750
00:44:30,801 --> 00:44:33,480
day reclaim its spot on the pedestal,

751
00:44:33,481 --> 00:44:35,680
overlooking this gift to the world.

